<div class="activity basic-app">
    <div class="activity-header">
        <span class="activity-label">In the Code</span>
        <span class="activity-time">~30 min</span>
    </div>

    <p>
        Two lookups and two decisions: what the Rigidbody2D offers, and the four ways a body can
        leave the ground.
    </p>

    <table>
        <tr><th>Field on the Rigidbody2D</th><th>What it actually does</th></tr>
        <tr>
            <td><code>Body Type</code></td>
            <td>Dynamic bodies fall and are pushed. Kinematic bodies move only where code puts them, and <strong>ignore gravity, forces and mass entirely</strong> — if your character stands still in mid-air with Gravity Scale 5, look here first.</td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
            <td><code>Gravity Scale</code></td>
            <td>A <strong>multiplier</strong> on the project-wide gravity (Project Settings, Physics 2D, Y at <code>-9.81</code>). 0 is off, 1 is undiluted. Means nothing on a Kinematic body.</td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
            <td><code>Mass</code></td>
            <td>How much the <em>same</em> force moves you. Gravity pulls harder on a heavy body, so mass cancels out in a fall — but a force you apply by hand divides by mass.</td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
            <td><code>Constraints</code></td>
            <td>Freeze Rotation Z, set in <a href="2.2.html">2.2</a> so the character stops tumbling. It matters in the air too: an unfrozen body spins when it clips a corner mid-jump.</td>
        </tr>
    </table>

    <table>
        <tr><th>Technique</th><th>What it requires</th><th>What it costs</th></tr>
        <tr>
            <td><code>rb.AddForceY(jumpForce, ForceMode2D.Impulse)</code></td>
            <td>A Dynamic body and Gravity Scale above 0.</td>
            <td>Mass divides into it, so retuning mass silently retunes every jump. Clean parabola — the row the walkthrough builds.</td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
            <td><code>rb.linearVelocityY = jumpSpeed</code></td>
            <td>A Dynamic body and Gravity Scale above 0.</td>
            <td>Identical height every time, to the pixel — and it erases whatever vertical motion was already there: a platform's lift, a knockback, a bounce.</td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
            <td><code>AddForceY(thrust, ForceMode2D.Force)</code> every frame while held</td>
            <td>A Dynamic body; some rule that stops it — fuel, ceiling, timer.</td>
            <td>A rocket, not a jump. Height depends on how long you hold — and this is what you get by accident if you forget the force mode.</td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
            <td><code>MovePosition()</code> in steps</td>
            <td>A <strong>Kinematic</strong> body, moved in <code>FixedUpdate()</code>.</td>
            <td>It ignores gravity, so it never falls, and it only collides with Dynamic bodies — so no floor is solid to it, and no <code>OnCollisionEnter2D(Collision2D collision)</code> fires against those static colliders. The cave door runs on that callback and would go silent.</td>
        </tr>
    </table>

    <div class="callout">
        <strong>Architectural specification: how does your script know it is in the cave?</strong>
        Your movement code assumes a top-down world, and the script has to be told which world it
        woke up in. All four options below are used in real projects; the walkthrough builds (a).
        <ul>
            <li><strong>(a) Ask the scene its name</strong> — <code>SceneManager.GetActiveScene().name</code> against <code>"Overworld"</code>. Readable, no setup; breaks silently if someone renames the scene. <em>This game's choice.</em></li>
            <li><strong>(b) Ask the scene its build index</strong> — the cave is index 2. Survives a rename; lies when the build order changes. <code>SceneSwitch.cs</code> already does this.</li>
            <li><strong>(c) A serialized bool, ticked per scene instance</strong> — visible in the Inspector without pressing Play. Costs a tick on every new Player somebody places, and a tick is easy to forget.</li>
            <li><strong>(d) A component that only exists on the cave's Player</strong> — the component's presence <em>is</em> the flag. Costs one more file and one more lookup.</li>
            <li>Whatever you pick, reuse a scene-mode flag you already built this year: two flags meaning "which world am I in" is how a project starts rotting.</li>
        </ul>
    </div>

    <div class="callout">
        <strong>Architectural specification: gravity and the flick.</strong>
        <ul>
            <li>Where does the cave's gravity get turned on? Name the exact point in the lifecycle — a field default, <code>Start()</code>, or every frame — and say what breaks at the other two.</li>
            <li>Which key, and which of <code>GetKey</code> or <code>GetKeyDown</code>? Say what holding the key for two seconds does under each.</li>
            <li>Is your jump force a serialized field or a magic number? Does its name say <em>force</em> or <em>speed</em> — and what does the name make the next reader believe?</li>
            <li>How do you know the jump worked? Name what you would print, and what it proves.</li>
        </ul>
    </div>

    <div class="socratic">
        <h3>Socratic: Gravity Is a Number You Own</h3>
        <ol>
            <li>Real gravity is a constant of the universe. Unity ships it as an <em>Inspector field</em>. Why would an engine make a law of physics editable per object?</li>
            <li>Same Player prefab, same script, two worlds: the hub needs Gravity Scale 0 and the cave needs it above 0. Where should that difference live — the script, the scene, or both?</li>
            <li>A Kinematic body ignores gravity, forces and mass entirely. Set Body Type to Kinematic in the cave, and what would your jump code have to do instead to look like a jump?</li>
        </ol>
    </div>
</div>

